// TEST: ML Galilean Ml Op Rejected (rejects)
// ERROR: BL4009
import ml as ml
// ============================================================================
// A BOOST-VARIANT VALUE ENTERING AN `ml.*` OP — the rule at
// MLGalilean.judgeApp's ml-alias arm, which was LIVE AND UNPINNED until this
// file (found by the retired galilean-layer census, §5).
//
// The ml ops are representation machinery: they take a vector to spherical
// harmonics, contract irreps, apply Schur-basis layers. All of that is defined
// on the VALUE it is handed, so handing it a velocity means the answer depends
// on the observer's frame — `y_to(1, u)` in one frame and `y_to(1, u + v)` in
// another are different harmonics, and no equivariance in the world relates
// them, because the boost is not a rotation. So the whole ml vocabulary is
// closed to boost-variant arguments and open to invariant ones: velocities
// enter a model only through boost-INVARIANT combinations — differences,
// sgs.grad, sgs.stress — which is what corpus 024/025 and sgs/013 pin from the
// accepting side.
//
// WHY IT WAS WORTH PINNING. This rule is galilean's "family C": its premise is
// the surface `ml.` alias, which is GONE after MLElaborate's pass 2, so a
// typed second opinion cannot see it at all (the census measures: abstain).
// That makes it exactly the rule most likely to be silently lost by a future
// refactor, and it had no corpus defence. The accepting twin below is the
// other half — the same op, the same certificate, an invariant argument.
// ============================================================================

// The rejection: `u` is the declared boost-variant parameter, and its
// components flow straight into an ml op. BL4009.
function bad(u: Array<Float like Idx<3>>) where ml.galilean(u) -> Float = {
    let sh = ml.y_to(1, u(0), u(1), u(2))
    sh(0)
}

let uv = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]
let out = bad(uv)
